04,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Ben Johnson"},{"season":2022,"team":"NO","distance":0.4482,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Pete Carmichael"},{"season":2022,"team":"DAL","distance":0.4895,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2019,"team":"IND","distance":0.4949,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Frank Reich"},{"season":2023,"team":"ARI","distance":0.5013,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Drew Petzing"}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"MIA","distance":0.5065,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.7922,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.8232,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2021,"team":"CHI","distance":0.8413,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CAR","distance":0.8688,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Ejiro Evero"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Broncos went 7-10 in 2021 and outscored opponents 335 to 322. The offense ranked 17th in EPA per play and the defense 18th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 2nd highest 13 personnel usage and the highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Broncos offense was about average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest 13 personnel usage and the 2nd lowest empty formation usage in the league. Teddy Bridgewater ran the passing game, with Courtland Sutton leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Broncos defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. Malik Reed was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Shelby Harris, who had 6 sacks despite playing only 56% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Broncos were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 84% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 17% of the money on the books.","players":"Teddy Bridgewater played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,052 yards. Targets were spread around, with Courtland Sutton and Noah Fant leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Javonte Williams and Melvin Gordon. On defense, Shelby Harris led the team with 6 sacks and Justin Simmons picked off 5 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Bears under Ben Johnson and the 2022 Saints under Pete Carmichael. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Dolphins under Vic Fangio and the 2021 Chargers under Brandon Staley.","trends":"The offense went from 31st in EPA per play in 2020 to 17th in 2021, a big step forward. The defense moved from 18th in EPA allowed per play in 2020 to 18th in 2021, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was two-high safety rate, which went from 18% to 86%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2021. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}